Putting my code next to the AI’s felt a little surreal. My sandwich plate is charming and personal, but simple, the interactivity is straightforward, and the code is easy to follow. The AI’s aquarium, on the other hand, was much more dynamic. The fish movement was fluid, the color palette was carefully chosen, and the system for spawning new fish made it feel alive in a way my sandwich never could.
I learned a lot by comparing the two. The AI’s code used arrays and object-oriented programming to manage the fish, while I was mostly using variables and simple functions. It pushed me to think about how I could structure my sketches to be more scalable, even for something as humble as a sandwich plate. On the other hand, my sketch told more of a personal story, I put my own spin on the interaction, and it felt like “me” in a way the AI’s didn’t.
Was using Gen AI helpful? Absolutely, for inspiration and for leveling up my technique. I picked up some new tricks, especially around event handling and animation. But it also made me realize that the human part, the story, the personality, is still where I can add the most value. The best work probably blends the two, technical skill (AI can help with that), and a personal perspective (that’s all me).
